### What is the broadcast state pattern?

The broadcast state pattern distributes a small, slowly-changing dataset to every parallel instance of an operator so they all hold an identical copy and apply it consistently. It is the standard Flink answer to "how do I change business rules in a running stream job without restarting it."

```java
import org.apache.flink.api.common.state.MapStateDescriptor;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.datastream.BroadcastStream;

// Step 1: Describe the broadcast state
MapStateDescriptor<String, FraudRule> ruleDescriptor =
    new MapStateDescriptor<>(
        "fraud-rules", String.class, FraudRule.class);

// Step 2: Mark the control stream as broadcast
BroadcastStream<FraudRule> broadcastRules =
    ruleStream.broadcast(ruleDescriptor);

// Step 3: Connect main stream to broadcast stream
txnStream
    .keyBy(txn -> txn.getAccountId())
    .connect(broadcastRules)
    .process(new FraudScreeningFunction())
    .uid("fraud-screening");
```

### How does the process function work?

The process function has two methods: `processBroadcastElement` handles broadcast records and is the only place that can modify the state, and `processElement` handles main records with read-only access to the state.

```java
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.functions.co.KeyedBroadcastProcessFunction;
import org.apache.flink.util.Collector;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class FraudScreeningFunction
        extends KeyedBroadcastProcessFunction<
            String, Transaction, FraudRule, String> {

    private final MapStateDescriptor<String, FraudRule> ruleDescriptor;

    public FraudScreeningFunction(
            MapStateDescriptor<String, FraudRule> ruleDescriptor) {
        this.ruleDescriptor = ruleDescriptor;
    }

    @Override
    public void processBroadcastElement(FraudRule rule,
            Context ctx, Collector<String> out) throws Exception {
        // WRITE: update broadcast state (allowed here)
        ctx.getBroadcastState(ruleDescriptor)
            .put(rule.getRuleId(), rule);
        // Every parallel instance gets this update
    }

    @Override
    public void processElement(Transaction txn,
            ReadOnlyContext ctx, Collector<String> out)
            throws Exception {
        // READ-ONLY: check transaction against rules
        for (FraudRule rule :
                ctx.getBroadcastState(ruleDescriptor).immutableEntries()) {
            if (rule.getValue().matches(txn)) {
                out.collect("ALERT: " + txn.getAccountId()
                    + " violated rule " + rule.getKey());
            }
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Broadcast rule update flow:
  Rule stream: {"ruleId":"R1","maxAmount":500000}
       │
       ▼ broadcast to ALL instances
  ┌──────────────┐  ┌──────────────┐  ┌──────────────┐
  │ Instance 0   │  │ Instance 1   │  │ Instance 2   │
  │ rules: {R1}  │  │ rules: {R1}  │  │ rules: {R1}  │
  │ (identical)  │  │ (identical)  │  │ (identical)  │
  └──────────────┘  └──────────────┘  └──────────────┘
       ▲                  ▲                  ▲
  Main transactions flow keyed by accountId
```

### Why is the main side read-only?

The read-only restriction on the main side is deliberate: if each instance could mutate the state based on its own partition's data, the copies would diverge and the pattern would break. Only the broadcast side writes, ensuring consistency.

```java
// processBroadcastElement: can WRITE broadcast state
ctx.getBroadcastState(ruleDescriptor).put(rule.getRuleId(), rule);

// processElement: READ-ONLY access
ctx.getBroadcastState(ruleDescriptor).immutableEntries();
// ctx.getBroadcastState(ruleDescriptor).put(...) → NOT ALLOWED
```

### What are the constraints?

The broadcast dataset must be small because it is fully replicated in memory on every instance. There is no strict ordering guarantee between broadcast updates and main records across instances.

```java
// GOOD: small rule set (tens to hundreds of rules)
MapStateDescriptor<String, FraudRule> ruleDesc =
    new MapStateDescriptor<>("rules", String.class, FraudRule.class);
// ~100 rules × 1 KB each = 100 KB per instance
// With 100 instances = 10 MB total (fine)

// BAD: large reference table
MapStateDescriptor<String, CustomerProfile> profileDesc =
    new MapStateDescriptor<>("profiles",
        String.class, CustomerProfile.class);
// 10 million profiles × 1 KB = 10 GB per instance!
// With 100 instances = 1 TB total (NOT suitable for broadcast)
// Use a lookup join or async I/O instead
```

### How do you use it for dynamic rule updates?

The key benefit is live rule updates without restarting the job. The risk team pushes a new rule onto the Kafka control topic and every instance picks it up immediately.

```java
// Control topic: nbi-fraud-rules (Kafka)
// Main topic: nbi-transactions (Kafka)

DataStream<FraudRule> ruleStream = env
    .fromSource(KafkaSource.<FraudRule>builder()
        .setBootstrapServers("broker1:9092")
        .setTopics("nbi-fraud-rules")
        .setDeserializer(new FraudRuleDeserializer())
        .build(),
        WatermarkStrategy.noWatermarks(),
        "fraud-rules-source");

// New rule published to Kafka:
// {"ruleId":"R99","type":"BLOCK_MERCHANT","merchantId":"7995"}
// → Every instance gets it → Next transaction to merchant 7995 is blocked
```

### How does it interact with checkpointing?

Broadcast state is checkpointed like any other operator state. On restore, every instance gets the full broadcast state, which matches its replicated nature.

```java
// Broadcast state is snapshotted in every checkpoint
// On restore: every instance gets the complete rule set
// No inconsistency between instances after recovery
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is broadcasting a large dataset, which replicates it to every instance and consumes enormous memory. Another is trying to modify broadcast state from the main processing side.

```java
// WRONG -- broadcasting a large reference table
BroadcastStream<CustomerProfile> broadcastProfiles =
    profileStream.broadcast(profileDescriptor);
// 10 million profiles replicated to every instance → OOM!

// CORRECT -- use async I/O or lookup join for large data
txnStream.keyBy(txn -> txn.getAccountId())
    .process(new AsyncLookupFunction());  // query DB per key
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Design broadcast rules to be safe to apply slightly before or after a given main record, since there is no strict ordering guarantee across instances. If strict coordination is needed, handle it explicitly in the process function. The broadcast state pattern is the standard answer for dynamic configuration, feature flags, and fraud rules in streaming systems.
